.TH VERLET 1 "2026-07-10" "Verlet" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
verlet \- run and inspect declared agent workloads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B verlet
.RI < command >
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B verlet help
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B verlet commands
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B verlet
is the local command-line surface for the Verlet runtime.
It starts browser and terminal consoles, authors and publishes agent artifacts,
runs tools and couplings, manages local credentials and secrets, and exposes
the runtime control plane.
.PP
Root help is intentionally short.
Use
.B verlet commands
for the canonical command list, or
.B verlet help
.I command
for a command's exact syntax and options.
.SH GETTING STARTED
.TP
.B verlet console
Start the bundled browser console on loopback and open it in the default
browser.
.TP
.B verlet chat
Start the bundled terminal console with a private local runtime.
.TP
.BI "verlet init " name
Create a folder-first agent project.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B console
Run the browser console and its local RPC endpoint.
.TP
.B chat
Run the terminal console, or attach it to an existing RPC endpoint.
.TP
.B init
Create an agent project. This is the short form of
.BR "verlet agent init" .
.TP
.B agent
Initialize, plan, publish, inspect, and run agent declarations.
.TP
.B coupling
Initialize a Wasm coupling package or replay a coupling against recorded
events without changing the source journal.
.TP
.B tool
Build, publish, inspect, and run operations. The
.B source
subcommands manage imported tool sources.
.TP
.B import
Build and publish operations from a witnessed external contract corpus.
.TP
.B skill
Publish a skill package to the local registry.
.TP
.B blob
Publish immutable file resources to the local registry.
.TP
.B auth
Store and inspect local model-provider credentials. Secret values are read
from standard input and are not printed.
.TP
.B secret
Store and inspect named runtime secrets. Secret values are read from the
environment or standard input and are not printed.
.TP
.B rpc
Run the public app-server control plane on a Unix socket or WebSocket
listener.
.TP
.B daemon
Run, validate, install, or remove the user-level daemon service.
.TP
.B debug
Inspect the RPC protocol of a running daemon. These commands are intended for
maintainers and smoke tests.
.TP
.B commands
Print the canonical command list.
.TP
.B help
Print root help or the help page for a command path.
.SH HELP
The following forms are equivalent for a command:
.PP
.nf
verlet help agent publish
verlet agent publish --help
.fi
.PP
Use
.B verlet commands
when exact command coverage matters. It is the exhaustive command inventory;
this manual is an orientation and reference overview.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VERLET_HOME
Override the default Verlet user directory.
.TP
.B VERLET_CONSOLE_ASSET_DIR
Use a built console asset directory containing
.I index.html
instead of the assets bundled with a release.
.PP
Provider-specific environment variables and environment-file settings are
described by
.B verlet help chat
and the daemon configuration documentation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.verlet/
Default user data, installed releases, metadata, and runtime state.
.TP
.I .verlet/
Default project-local registries and state.
.TP
.I verlet.toml
Default daemon and runtime configuration file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B verlet
returns zero when a command succeeds and nonzero for invalid usage, rejected
operations, or runtime failures. Diagnostics are written to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation:
